WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.416 --> 00:00:08.217
Hello everyone!

2
00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:20.841
Welcome back to Superstars in History, the talk show featuring famous people from the past.

3
00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.222
I'm your host, Faye Moss.

4
00:00:24.002 --> 00:00:30.384
Right now I'm dying for some chocolate, and it's all the fault of our guest today!

5
00:00:31.648 --> 00:00:39.194
He's the man who introduced Americans to chocolate, and he's famous for his philanthropy and the town that

6
00:00:39.214 --> 00:00:40.055
bears his name.

7
00:00:41.196 --> 00:00:45.800
Straight from history, please welcome Milton Hershey!

8
00:01:01.068 --> 00:01:02.169
Hello, Faye.

9
00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:05.011
May I offer you a Hershey's bar?

10
00:01:07.814 --> 00:01:08.614
Thanks, Mr.

11
00:01:08.674 --> 00:01:14.559
Hershey! I can't eat during the show, so let's get this interview started!

12
00:01:14.579 --> 00:01:18.983
As a kid, did you dream of owning a chocolate factory?

13
00:01:19.003 --> 00:01:20.524
Not at all.

14
00:01:21.945 --> 00:01:29.892
When I was born in rural Pennsylvania in 1857, most Americans hadn't even tasted chocolate.

15
00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:37.442
In the mid-nineteenth century, "candy" meant treats like taffy, caramels, and hard candies.

16
00:01:38.463 --> 00:01:41.505
I got in the business purely by accident.

17
00:01:42.326 --> 00:01:44.088
That sounds intriguing!

18
00:01:44.108 --> 00:01:45.489
Tell us more.

19
00:01:46.389 --> 00:01:51.694
My family moved around a lot because my father had trouble holding a job.

20
00:01:52.635 --> 00:01:58.039
I was never able to attend one school for very long, and by the time I was 14,

21
00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:02.082
my mother thought it would be better for me to work.

22
00:02:02.862 --> 00:02:08.506
So I quit school and became a printer's apprentice, but I hated that job.

23
00:02:09.407 --> 00:02:14.010
One day I decided to let my straw hat fall into a printing press.

24
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:18.012
The press stopped working, and I got fired.

25
00:02:19.453 --> 00:02:21.675
Did you find a job in a candy factory?

26
00:02:22.455 --> 00:02:23.476
Not a factory.

27
00:02:23.972 --> 00:02:30.396
I spent the next four years at an ice cream and candy shop in Lancaster, Pennsylvania.

28
00:02:32.357 --> 00:02:39.102
I was good at making candy and learned so much that I opened my own shop in Philadelphia.

29
00:02:39.762 --> 00:02:48.548
It was 1876, and many people were visiting the Centennial Exposition—the first world's fair in the United States.

30
00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:57.244
I ran a pipe outside to tempt customers with the delicious smells of candy-making.

31
00:02:57.825 --> 00:03:01.126
Lots of them stopped in the shop on their way to the fair.

32
00:03:02.667 --> 00:03:09.631
As time passed, I expanded into the wholesale business and moved to a bigger shop.

33
00:03:10.512 --> 00:03:11.973
It sounds like you were a success.

34
00:03:12.013 --> 00:03:14.294
Yes, for half-a-dozen years.

35
00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.644
But then I made some bad decisions and had to close my business.

36
00:03:23.525 --> 00:03:27.428
The next year I opened another shop in New York City.

37
00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:32.532
When that one failed, I was forced to return to Lancaster.

38
00:03:33.353 --> 00:03:37.917
Maybe I'd have been more successful if I'd had more education.

39
00:03:38.798 --> 00:03:42.040
I'm sure your family was delighted to see you.

40
00:03:42.781 --> 00:03:45.844
Not delighted enough to help me start a new business!

41
00:03:46.652 --> 00:03:54.859
My mother's family had financed my Philadelphia and New York stores, but by now everyone thought I was

42
00:03:54.959 --> 00:03:57.561
as irresponsible as my father.

43
00:03:58.482 --> 00:04:02.545
I had to start my new venture—making caramels—on my own.

44
00:04:02.565 --> 00:04:09.430
When I received a check for a big order, I was so excited I ran to the bank

45
00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:10.851
wearing my apron.

46
00:04:12.192 --> 00:04:16.535
My factory eventually employed over 700 people.

47
00:04:17.355 --> 00:04:19.876
What motivated you to start making chocolate?

48
00:04:20.737 --> 00:04:25.299
With the success of my caramel factory, I had money to travel.

49
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:33.104
In Europe I tasted the finest Swiss milk chocolate—an expensive luxury.

50
00:04:33.924 --> 00:04:40.408
Unlike American chocolate, which was bitter and brittle, this was sweet and creamy.

51
00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:48.762
But the real turning point came in 1893, when I attended another world's fair in Chicago.

52
00:04:49.482 --> 00:04:58.025
A German company's exhibit demonstrated chocolate making—from roasting cacao beans to the finished product—right there.

53
00:04:59.125 --> 00:05:03.606
I kept returning to watch the machinery and sample the chocolate.

54
00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:08.008
I believed chocolate had a bigger future than caramels.

55
00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:15.779
When the fair ended, I purchased equipment from the exhibit and started a product line that included chocolate

56
00:05:15.839 --> 00:05:17.219
coatings and cocoa.

57
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.441
Did you also make a milk chocolate bar?

58
00:05:21.381 --> 00:05:21.921
Not yet.

59
00:05:22.642 --> 00:05:28.604
No one in America had made milk chocolate before, so I had to figure it out myself.

60
00:05:29.525 --> 00:05:33.847
Fortunately I had always enjoyed experimenting with recipes.

61
00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:42.114
In 1900 I sold my caramel company for a million dollars to focus on chocolate.

62
00:05:42.835 --> 00:05:50.658
The first Hershey's milk chocolate was produced that year but could only be sold locally because it spoiled

63
00:05:50.758 --> 00:05:56.180
quickly. I hadn't figured out how to keep milk chocolate from spoiling yet.

64
00:05:56.881 --> 00:06:04.104
With a small group of employees, I worked night and day for several more years on this problem.

65
00:06:04.812 --> 00:06:08.474
We experimented with different ways of condensing milk.

66
00:06:09.234 --> 00:06:17.457
Finally we discovered a method that allowed us to mass-produce long-lasting chocolate bars at a price everyone could

67
00:06:17.497 --> 00:06:19.358
afford: five cents.

68
00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:23.020
And they stayed a nickel for many years.

69
00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.482
Even before you had the perfect recipe, you were confident you'd succeed.

70
00:06:29.222 --> 00:06:34.842
You had already started building a giant chocolate factory—and a town for the workers!

71
00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:36.423
That's right.

72
00:06:37.283 --> 00:06:45.209
I began construction on the world's largest chocolate factory in 1903, and it opened two years later.

73
00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:53.075
I wanted my employees to have a good place to live, so I oversaw every detail, including naming

74
00:06:53.115 --> 00:06:53.656
the streets.

75
00:06:54.616 --> 00:07:03.186
The main streets intersected at Chocolate and Cocoa Avenues, and other streets were named after cacao-growing areas.

76
00:07:03.966 --> 00:07:11.271
The houses were high quality and had indoor plumbing and electricity before many other American homes did.

77
00:07:12.471 --> 00:07:16.433
Hershey, Pennsylvania even became a tourist attraction!

78
00:07:17.374 --> 00:07:21.636
Yes, that's because Hershey was more than a factory town.

79
00:07:22.357 --> 00:07:25.358
There were parks, theaters, and even a zoo!

80
00:07:26.119 --> 00:07:27.880
Tell us about the school you founded.

81
00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:30.882
It was my dear wife's idea.

82
00:07:31.683 --> 00:07:35.706
Kitty was always sick, and we were unable to have children.

83
00:07:36.767 --> 00:07:41.091
In 1909 we founded a residential school for orphan boys.

84
00:07:42.052 --> 00:07:49.258
Unlike most orphanages where the children lived in dorms, our boys lived in cottages with house parents.

85
00:07:50.259 --> 00:07:55.724
In addition to their schoolwork, they were expected to learn a trade and milk cows.

86
00:07:56.476 --> 00:07:58.637
A chocolate factory needs lots of milk!

87
00:07:59.457 --> 00:08:02.399
It sounds like the school was really important to you.

88
00:08:03.339 --> 00:08:06.940
I wanted other boys to have the stability I never had.

89
00:08:07.961 --> 00:08:15.564
In 1918, three years after Kitty died, I donated my multi-million-dollar fortune to the school.

90
00:08:16.304 --> 00:08:21.847
Because I had no children, I decided the Hershey boys would become my heirs.

91
00:08:23.047 --> 00:08:28.653
Thank you for sharing your story today—I'll think of you every time I eat chocolate!

92
00:08:29.373 --> 00:08:33.454
We'll let you get back to history now, and I'll enjoy the treat you brought me!

93
00:08:34.395 --> 00:08:35.455
Thank you, Faye.

94
00:08:39.516 --> 00:08:45.818
Milton Hershey continued to take an interest in the chocolate business and the school until he died in

95
00:08:45.898 --> 00:08:54.930
1945. Originally only white male orphans were enrolled at the Milton Hershey School, but by the mid-twentieth century,

96
00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:58.794
the school was welcoming all low-income boys and girls.

97
00:08:59.535 --> 00:09:08.624
The town, which features street lights shaped like Hershey's Kisses and smells like chocolate, still draws tourists, especially

98
00:09:08.724 --> 00:09:09.965
to its amusement park.

99
00:09:10.786 --> 00:09:11.887
That's all for today.

100
00:09:12.387 --> 00:09:16.031
See you next time on Superstars in History!
